What:		/sys/bus/virtio/devices/<dev>/no_alloc
Date:		August 2026
Contact:	virtualization@lists.linux.dev
Description:
		Read-only.  1 means the host has told this device it can no
		longer stand behind the memory it has plugged, and the guest
		has stopped allocating from it.

		This is host state, not guest policy, which is why it cannot
		be written.  A guest able to set it could withdraw memory the
		host never asked to withdraw; a guest able to clear it could
		keep allocating memory the host has already said it cannot
		back.  The host sets it through the device's configuration
		space (VIRTIO_MEM_F_NO_ALLOC).

		It is exposed so an administrator can see *why* the guest is
		under pressure.  A 1 here means allocations from this memory
		fail rather than being backed, and virtio-balloon will not
		deflate into it to rescue an OOM -- so a guest that is being
		OOM-killed while apparently holding free memory is a guest
		whose host is over-committed, not a guest with a leak.

		Existing residents are untouched: nothing is migrated or
		reclaimed on account of the withdrawal, and unplug continues
		to work normally.

		The unit is the ZONE_MOVABLE of the device's NUMA node, not
		the device, so this is only meaningful where the device owns
		that node's movable memory.  Memory this device onlined to
		ZONE_NORMAL rather than ZONE_MOVABLE is not covered, and
		reads 0 regardless of what the host asked for.
Users:		virtio-mem management tooling
